  {
    "episode_id": 92293,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92293.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think it is. You know, the problem with President Biden is he doesn't seem that dangerous when you're looking at him or talking to him or listening to him until you really begin to think about all the things he said. And it's pretty overwhelming. I mean, this would be in last night's address, there was basically $4 trillion. of new spending. And that doesn't count the $2.3 trillion that supposedly was to fight COVID with very little of it going to anything that had anything to do with COVID. Very little to vaccines, very little to treatment, a lot of that money not being spent until 2000, even as late as 2028 or so. And then last night, We heard all of these new things that the government needed to do that the government's never done before. And literally from the beginning to the end, there is something there for everybody on the education front. Two more years at either end of the education that government has traditionally taken a responsibility for. But of course, most importantly, that's traditionally been the responsibility of the government closest to you. Most of us believe that when you go from the local school board to Jefferson City, you may have gotten too far away to make the kind of decisions that need to be made for kids and families where you live. And, you know, last night we heard we need to have kindergarten for three-year-olds and four-year-olds. So, you know, that would start school. The school would start two years earlier and then free community college education. Now, my view would be if you really want to make education expensive, make it free. it will really cost a lot if the government decides that the government's gonna pay for it and everybody making the decision locally about every issue is well this is really the federal government's gonna do this so we should we no longer need to think about the most efficient way to do this let's think about the way that is the quickest way to do it or the way we want to spend the most money on the community college level i'm a big advocate for community colleges i think as the president did say last night, the kind of jobs that are out there that associates degree is important. I'd even argue and have been arguing that stackable credentials that may or may not lead to an associate's degree are even more important than that degree. And certainly if they nicely stack up to where after you've got a couple of years of them under your belt, You get a degree as part of that. That's a good thing. It's not a bad thing. But it's that credential that leads you to a job in drafting or in some kind of engineering work or in welding or HVAC, the kind of good jobs that are out there that people need to be thinking about are the kinds of things that our community college network is really good at. And if you qualify for the full Pell Grant, which means you're in that group that we've decided needs the most help, There's not a community college in Missouri and I don't believe there's a community college in America where the full Pell Grant doesn't pay all tuition, all fees, all books with a little money left over at the end of that Pell Grant. So if that's true, the people that are in the most in need already have for the first two years of higher education after high school. We're going to have to look at this very carefully. This is more money in the first 90 days, the first 100 days, I guess, of the administrations being proposed to be spent than we may have spent in half a decade before the year we're in right now. And how are they going to pay for it? going to pay for it by reversing what I think were the good decisions we made in the 2017 tax bill, the corporate rate that got us to about the midpoint of corporate rates around the world. And we can compete at that level, though, and what we've done since I've been in the Congress working year after year to try to get the inheritance tax to where it made sense where you weren't being taxed twice on the things you'd taken a lifetime to put together for businesses and family farms that inherit this exemption at 11 million covers almost everybody. One of my colleagues, Chris Van Holland from Maryland, is introduced to Bill to take that exemption back to a million dollars. and to have a step up basis so that you can inherit at what value you can establish that the person you were inheriting it from put into it. But the difference in that and what it's worth the day you inherited it would be tax at a capital gains rate. And by the way, for some Americans, he said the capital gains rate now should go from 23 or so percent to about 43%. The more you look at what was said yesterday, the more I think we need to be concerned about a system where you do reward work, you reward cost and benefit are important things. And so we're going to have to work really hard, I think, to be sure that we, one, keep in the Senate, keep the 60 vote threshold in place. which prevents us from with 50 Democrats and the vice president doing whatever they want to do and two to really be sure people understand what's at stake here. I think you can almost, almost imagine that there'll be a return of the waters of the US rule or something very much like it. Nobody anywhere in the country, I think, bought that harder than Missouri Farm Bureau did. And you know, Gareth, the times of many times I took that Farm Bureau map to the floor of the Senate that pointed out that like 99% of our state would have been basically under the control of the EPA for a building permit, the ability to put it a sidewalk, maybe the ability to mow the right away, possibly the ability of what you can put on your farm field. That's the moment we're headed back toward, and we need to be sure that people understand what's at stake and that the people that work for you here in Washington are hearing from you that we need to re-engage in the successful fight we had a handful of years ago, but most of those issues have come right back. And the final thing I'll say, they've come right back with an administration that has the smallest margin Democrats have had in the House since the Civil War. And we had a slightly smaller margin one time when I was the whip in the house. But this is a very, this is a four or five vote turnaround margin, which in the house is no margin at all. The Senate couldn't possibly be closer at 50 and 50. And frankly, the presidential election was not, there was no mandate asked for. The President Trump wanted the election to be about him and President Biden wanted the election to be about President Trump. And so it turned into a very narrow referendum. where President Trump did much better than any poll had suggested, but at the end of the day, it didn't win. And now they wanna talk about the mandate and if they can find a Republican anywhere that's for what they're for, not in the Congress, but anywhere, suddenly, well, it's a bipartisan proposal because a lot of Republicans in the polling say they're for it. And my view would be generally that's because they don't know what's in it. It's like the infrastructure bill that doesn't have much to do with infrastructure and has to do with a lot of the things the president talked about last night.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
