    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92293.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "I think there was an expectation that President Biden would reach out more than he has so far that this would be a more inclusive process and we're just not seeing that. Somebody said last night after the speech Bernie Sanders must have been really happy with that speech and The climate issues the president specifically talked about There are things that we're doing. There's some legislation out there that I've recently joined that the American Farm Bureau is for that wants to look at some of the realities of climate change, but there's a way to do that without ending your ability to do what you do or ending our ability to be competitive. Now, the president did point out last night that we have a very small impact on the worldwide climate. Now, he used that as a reason to just getting back into the Paris agreement But the more important point is that we can do everything we could possibly do. And as the president himself said, it won't have much impact on any climate change that's going on or any of the things we want to do. We're seeing substantial benefits by our efforts at home and at the farm and in the utility providers of the country to try to change things to where the climate is in better shape and the air quality is in better shape and the water quality is in better shape. And what I've told the administration a couple of times is on almost all of these issues, if you give people enough time, they're going to try to do what everybody believes is the right thing. But if you try to do it in In 10 years, as opposed to 30 years, people can't afford to pay the bill. It has incredible impact on their economy. And we don't get a chance to have the worldwide impact we want to have. as a leader on these issues and we are leader on these issues. We've always been a leader on these issues. One of the reasons that signing up to the Paris agreement is a bad idea is that we'll do what we say we're going to do and other countries won't. And that puts us at a disadvantage in every competition in the world. At the same time, Gary, as we talked about before, there are great opportunities out there. The worldwide food need,",
