    "turn_text": "and seeing what we can do to to raise a more competitive crop and a better crop on the same amount of land. That's why, again, where we live, infrastructure becomes so critically important. There's nobody better located anywhere in the world to be part of that worldwide market than Missouri, or Missouri farmers on the production side. And we're in a place where you can have the production side and also have the value added side and put that in a transportation system that should go anywhere in the world. Get it on water when you can get it on water, put it on rail when it needs to be on rail. be on the highways when that's the best place to be. And so obviously we need a competitive marketplace, but we don't need to be put out of that marketplace by making changes quicker than they need to be made or realistically can be made. Jobs will be lost and opportunities will be lost if we don't take a reasonable approach to a future that we have lots of reasons to be excited about and why we want to make that future needlessly difficult for us is something that I think a lot of people have to begin to ask that question. as they think about the message they send to Washington and Jefferson City and the county courthouse and the city hall.",
