  {
    "episode_id": 92293,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92293.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I don't feel quite as fresh as you may have announced me as feeling, but it's an exciting time in Jeff City. It really is. The legislature actually just left town for the week after wrapping up. Well, the Senate had a big week where they wrapped up their version of the budget. We know that's a big deal where the budget deadline is coming up Friday of next week. So that's kind of been the biggest topic of conversation probably from the larger scale. But it's been a busy week for us too. I want to start talking about our number one priority which is imminent domain reform, talking about the use of imminent domain for merchant transmission lines. We had a huge rally about a week ago, and I want to thank everybody who came out to show their support. I know we had kind of short notice on that, but really appreciate everybody coming out and showing their support for an issue that was big for us. I showed the support to the Senators the day of the hearing in the Senate Committee. So really did come out strong and had a lot of support from senators too. So we appreciate them. House bill handlers statewide leaders coming out and showing their support for an issue that's near and dear to all of our members. And especially those members who would be impacted by the project. So excited about that and really appreciate everybody coming out. Newest issues on that bill. I think we're still in the same position. You know the House bill is in the Senate. But I think our number one opportunity is going to be on a House bill on a Senate bill that comes back from the House. I think that's going to be our opportunity where the houses pass this issue a number of times to expect them to continue to do so and the Senate to be asked to take up that issue on a separate bill when it comes over from the house. So excited about that and expect to see some more information on that here in the next couple of weeks. I think we got a couple of weeks here and we're going to see an opportunity before session adjourns. Another issue that's been big for Farm Bureau this year is talking about infrastructure. As you know, when we talk about infrastructure, it's transportation and broadband. On the transportation front, we had positive movement this week with the House Transportation Committee voting the transportation funding bill due pass by overwhelming. Actually, it was unanimous. Everybody was in support. I think it was 13 to zero. So excited to see that unanimous support. That bill now will have to go through the House Rules Committee before it can proceed to the House floor. And there are some inside negotiations going on between House leadership and Senate leadership about moving that bill. The Senate is overwhelmingly supportive. And there's some on the House that are the same way, but there's going to be some work done to get that bill across the finish line. Farm Bureau continues to be completely engaged. in support of that. Farm Bureau members for a long time have been supportive of additional funding for transportation and we think this is a good way to do it. It's a stepped up or phased in fuel tax increase that includes a rebate option for those looking to get the rebate. So excited about that and a good opportunity here to address an issue that's been hanging out there for some time leaving Missouri with I think the 49th lowest fuel tax and very low funding for our transportation system overall. on the broadband front. We talked about budget to open things up. This Senate Appropriations Committee included $5 million for the broadband grant program. We appreciate them doing so. Unfortunately, when the house passed their budget, they were at zero. So that will be a conferenceable item when they go to conference next week. And we'll see where that plays out. I'll be honest with you though, either way, we're excited about the opportunities for broadband. Um, with some of the latest stimulus funds, there's going to be a lot of money coming to the state for broadband. We're going to get more information about that here in the coming weeks, but we know there's a substantial amount of money coming that's going to work through the state's office of broadband to help bring new connections. So we hope to see the state continue to be engaged in this, but either way, we expect large, um, large steps forward in the broadband front. There's been a couple of bills related to broadband, including to allow communities to come together to facilitate broadband deployment. And those are still going through the system. Actually, there was a hearing on it just this morning with just a couple of weeks left. I'm not sure if those make it across the finish line or not, but we're hopeful that they will. And we're looking for all opportunities to enhance broadband deployment across the state, while also making sure that we're being mindful of investments being made and making sure we're making those in a smart way. So I think there's some great opportunities there as well. On the Ag front, we've been talking about a lot of things throughout the year. One of the top of the list is using more biodiesel in the state. And that issue continues to evolve. The most recent is that now we're looking at an incentive program for those selling higher blend volumes of biodiesel across the state. The state would have an incentive program for them to ask them to do so. So rather than a mandate looking at incentivizing that type of activity and really expected to have a major impact, that has actually passed on the house on what we've put together as kind of an omnibus ag bill this week. and has an opportunity now. The Senate still hasn't debated that issue, but we expect them to take that up here in the next week to really have an issue of that or look at that issue. Finally, something we've been asking for some time is let's get some floor debate on this and actually see where everybody stands. So I think we have an opportunity here, and with this creative idea of not a mandate, but incentivizing that behavior may be an opportunity to use more soy, and have more biodiesel here in the state, and a great opportunity for everyone. So I think that's where we're headed on that issue, at least for the rest of this session, and I'm really excited about the opportunities for that. A couple of other bills that you've heard us talk about throughout are relating to Masbida, the State Department of Agriculture's tax credit programs that have helped with agricultural issues across the state. And those were included in that same omnibus bill, as well as a house bill that's in the Senate that just needs to be debated on the Senate floor. So I think we're well positioned for that. And then upgrading our private pesticide applicator training program I think we've had that on several different bills and well positioned as well. It's interesting this time of year the laundry list gets long and things are in a lot of places but I do think for both of those issues and for most of these issues we're well positioned here for the last couple of weeks. I hate the fact that I don't have a checklist of things that are completed yet But that's kind of the way things work in the Capitol and we're getting closer for a lot of these things. And especially with the Senate bill, it was actually Senate bill 37 has, if not all, almost all of these issues included in it and what would be an omnibus agriculture bill, it may be an opportunity to just take up and get a lot of this done in one swoop. So we're looking forward to that. It passed the House this week and is now in the Senate. The Senate theoretically could take it up and pass it or we could go to conference and work out any issues between the two. but hopefully we're able to take that up and get those all done at one time. And lastly, an issue that we haven't talked a lot about in the public, or a lot about, I don't know that we have on these this year, is feral hogs. And I know in those people that are impacted by these issues in their areas of the state, that's very important to them. It's something Farm Bureau has been paying attention to for some time. We actually had the house pass, a house bill related to feral hogs just today, right before I ran back over here. passed a compromise version of a feral hog bill that would update the definition of feral hogs and increase penalties for releasing or holding feral hogs. But it's a compromise position that we worked with all interested parties in. And I think we're in a place where we can actually see this get done this year. We're really excited about it. The how the bill is now over to the Senate for them to consider. And the Senate is teed up to take it up and get it moving quickly. So I think we have a real opportunity there to address an issue that's been important to our members from across the state. for some time. And like I said, this is a compromised position where we've worked with all the interested parties. And I think everybody will come out of this thinking we've addressed it in a good way. So I think that's kind of the update in as fast a form as I can give.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
