  {
    "episode_id": 92293,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92293.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Okay, thanks, Spencer. I'll give members just a couple more minutes to submit any questions that they may have before we wrap up, but I'd be remiss if I didn't comment about the hearing yesterday in which we had the opportunity to testify on the importance of rural broadband. So this was a hearing of a subcommittee of the House Transportation Infrastructure Committee. The hearing's purpose was to discuss the reauthorization of the economic development administration within the US Department of Commerce. American Farm Bureau was asked to testify specifically to broadband and its importance to agriculture and rural communities. We had the opportunity to provide testimony about its importance to agriculture, to entrepreneurship, small business, and healthcare really again using the opportunity to stress the symbiotic relationship between our farm and ranch families. and the communities in which we live and serve, and to draw out the point that broadband really is this invisible thread that connects all of us, particularly as we think about economic vitality moving forward and how we try to revitalize our communities. It's one important piece of the puzzle. So it was a good opportunity to, again, to talk about not just the importance, but also to stress to stress the importance of increasing the accuracy of mapping. So continued implementation of the broadband data act that was put in place by Congress and to talk a little bit about and trumpet some of the important actions that have been taken in Missouri as well that maybe are blueprints for other states. We've certainly learned from other states and brought back here some of the best practices for engaging in and deploying broadband. So Those are a few things on my mind from the hearing. Did I miss anything, Spencer?",
